Ask who may take address space.

A process under a limit on its address space keeps some of it aside, so that
a request that cannot fail still finds memory when the rest has been taken.
What it lets go of for such a request is free for every thread: a large
request of another thread, which could have been refused, takes it first.

mi_register_address_space_keeper() names two functions of the embedder.
may_take() is asked before address space is taken from the system for a page
of blocks larger than those of a small page, and can refuse it. Small pages,
and what mimalloc takes for its own records, are not asked for. make_room() is
asked when a request has found no memory: the request looks again for as long
as the answer is true.

A request that looks again takes no medium or large page of the usual size
(512 KiB, 4 MiB) but one that holds two blocks.

Nothing is asked where a request finds memory in the pages that are there.

